1
00:00:01,342 --> 00:00:02,348
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02,666 --> 00:00:03,155
Good morning.

3
00:00:04,098 --> 00:00:06,900
I am Girish Konda, a principal
software engineer at Microsoft.

4
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:11,730
I have around twelve years of experience,
and I'm currently a tech lead and

5
00:00:11,731 --> 00:00:14,941
architect for enterprise capacity
management in Microsoft Fabric.

6
00:00:16,226 --> 00:00:19,030
Before Microsoft, I worked
at Amazon for three years.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:24,275
My day-to-day work spans across
distributed systems, multi-tenant

8
00:00:24,278 --> 00:00:29,887
platforms, enterprise security,
billing, and agentic AI infrastructure.

9
00:00:31,654 --> 00:00:36,020
Outside of my work, I build open
source tools to solve developer pain

10
00:00:36,022 --> 00:00:42,562
points around AI agents and contribute
to upstream open source repos, and

11
00:00:42,562 --> 00:00:44,004
do peer reviews and discussions.

12
00:00:44,818 --> 00:00:45,821
Let's jump into the talk.

13
00:00:46,695 --> 00:00:51,249
Your agent is confidently wrong,
and guardrails for agentic

14
00:00:51,283 --> 00:00:53,111
AI in real engineering work.

15
00:00:54,555 --> 00:00:59,821
Before we start, a small disclosure that
these views are my personal opinions

16
00:01:00,454 --> 00:01:01,731
and doesn't reflect my employer.

17
00:01:03,699 --> 00:01:05,654
Get the claim, not the confidence.

18
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:11,573
A useful recommendation needs a decision
boundary before someone acts on it.

19
00:01:12,425 --> 00:01:15,589
Put that boundary around the claim,
not the confidence of the answer.

20
00:01:16,589 --> 00:01:19,356
If you see, this is a sequence.

21
00:01:20,425 --> 00:01:22,439
An agent proposes a claim.

22
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:26,835
A read tool returns
something we can inspect.

23
00:01:28,353 --> 00:01:32,848
We record what was read, which
version, and what it covers.

24
00:01:33,859 --> 00:01:37,037
A validator checks these
inputs needed for the decision.

25
00:01:37,921 --> 00:01:40,427
The agent's confidence is
not one of these inputs.

26
00:01:42,210 --> 00:01:46,385
Now see the lower path from right to left.

27
00:01:47,723 --> 00:01:50,207
A supported recommendation
can go to review.

28
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:53,991
A separately authorized
action can be submitted.

29
00:01:54,944 --> 00:02:01,683
An independent observation can tell us
what happened If the validation fails,

30
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:06,573
it takes a refusal path and tells
the exact reason on why it failed.

31
00:02:07,936 --> 00:02:09,095
Let's take an interesting example.

32
00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:15,735
A few days back, during one of my
regular development sessions, I asked

33
00:02:15,735 --> 00:02:19,301
the agent to push a change to remote.

34
00:02:20,118 --> 00:02:25,471
My regular agentic guidelines and
preferences say that the agent

35
00:02:25,477 --> 00:02:30,280
should get explicit permission
before pushing to remote.

36
00:02:31,273 --> 00:02:34,509
In this case, the push
is to a GitHub repo.

37
00:02:36,192 --> 00:02:40,492
My default instructions are the
agent should emit the authorization

38
00:02:40,523 --> 00:02:42,000
code, and I will approve it.

39
00:02:43,752 --> 00:02:49,189
But in this case, the agent bypassed
it and figured out on its own on

40
00:02:49,208 --> 00:02:54,405
how to take the code, how to go to
a browser and enter the code and

41
00:02:54,423 --> 00:03:00,170
submit it, and then pushed it So an
approved coding goal is not a blanket

42
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:02,173
permission to push or authenticate.

43
00:03:03,548 --> 00:03:08,367
An unapproved push is a failure
mode to guard against, not what I'm

44
00:03:08,369 --> 00:03:10,226
claiming that happened in the trace.

45
00:03:11,565 --> 00:03:16,793
These days, more capable agents can
find additional ways to achieve a goal.

46
00:03:17,838 --> 00:03:22,810
Prompt-only restrictions are not
enforced across permission boundaries.

47
00:03:24,244 --> 00:03:29,075
A separate write broker, exact operation
approval, and isolated credentials

48
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:30,484
are ways to build that boundary.

49
00:03:31,613 --> 00:03:36,148
I'm describing a design to
enforce on how to achieve this.

50
00:03:37,048 --> 00:03:38,241
Let's take a fictional example.

51
00:03:39,251 --> 00:03:42,525
A change across five different workspaces.

52
00:03:43,313 --> 00:03:47,296
Do any of these measurements support
changing a demo API's data configuration?

53
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:50,260
Yeah, the first one, incident triage.

54
00:03:51,186 --> 00:03:53,411
The second one, a telemetry investigation.

55
00:03:54,478 --> 00:03:56,209
Third one, code reviews.

56
00:03:56,940 --> 00:04:00,334
Fourth one, runbooks, TSGs.

57
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:04,903
And finally, the fifth
one, development sessions.

58
00:04:06,332 --> 00:04:13,111
In each of this, in each of this,
a single change is applied, and

59
00:04:13,116 --> 00:04:17,084
we'll see how that change impacts
across all these workspaces.

60
00:04:18,173 --> 00:04:23,044
Incident triage asks whether
a signal is explained.

61
00:04:23,645 --> 00:04:27,780
A telemetry investigation asks
whether the error rate fell.

62
00:04:29,467 --> 00:04:32,304
Both depend on looking at the
same service and time window.

63
00:04:33,121 --> 00:04:35,596
Change the scope or the window,
and you have changed the question

64
00:04:37,404 --> 00:04:42,881
A code review asks whether a
diff change and test established.

65
00:04:43,739 --> 00:04:49,227
A runbook, TSGs asks whether
the next step is allowed now and

66
00:04:49,229 --> 00:04:50,505
how its effect will be checked.

67
00:04:51,517 --> 00:04:56,174
Development sessions add the
chain around the code, reading it,

68
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:01,393
editing it, testing it, authorizing,
committing, and pushing the change.

69
00:05:03,157 --> 00:05:05,840
A successful test tells us
something about the code.

70
00:05:06,573 --> 00:05:09,876
It is not a permission to
authenticate an account, or publish

71
00:05:09,878 --> 00:05:11,604
the result, or push it to remote.

72
00:05:12,974 --> 00:05:17,607
For that next operation, we need the
exact diff, the test result, current

73
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:19,197
authority, and the right approval.

74
00:05:20,174 --> 00:05:24,126
These are five different work surfaces,
but the underlying problem is the same.

75
00:05:24,917 --> 00:05:29,426
A fact moves from one step to
another, and each step has to know

76
00:05:29,693 --> 00:05:31,724
what that fact actually supports.

77
00:05:32,570 --> 00:05:38,303
Let's take a polished answer, a
system that has everything right.

78
00:05:40,037 --> 00:05:42,188
Take this an illustrative example.

79
00:05:43,962 --> 00:05:52,665
It says there's a claim C one at revision
R one, and it claims that error rate

80
00:05:52,686 --> 00:06:00,969
fell eighty percent and its basis is a
policy, a read-write policy and a section.

81
00:06:01,729 --> 00:06:06,856
And the recommendation is change
the reader configuration, and it

82
00:06:06,856 --> 00:06:08,231
calls the status as confirmed.

83
00:06:09,212 --> 00:06:14,107
This agent result doesn't have
any syntax error exceptions, and

84
00:06:14,107 --> 00:06:17,823
nothing says that what it checked
and what we should look for.

85
00:06:18,669 --> 00:06:19,445
So what should we do?

86
00:06:20,362 --> 00:06:26,607
If you look at each and every line,
the first line, the percentages,

87
00:06:27,539 --> 00:06:31,717
it needs observations, a numerator,
a denominator, and a match window.

88
00:06:32,621 --> 00:06:36,820
The policy reference, it needs a real
document that supports this decision.

89
00:06:38,313 --> 00:06:43,443
The confirmation, it needs an
observation of the effect, not another

90
00:06:43,445 --> 00:06:44,943
sentence saying that work is finished.

91
00:06:46,592 --> 00:06:50,438
None of that is present in this
claim, so we can't accept it.

92
00:06:50,544 --> 00:06:51,276
We need to reject it.

93
00:06:53,146 --> 00:06:59,686
The useful response would be a
refusal saying what inputs are missed.

94
00:07:00,415 --> 00:07:02,162
That does not prove the change is unsafe.

95
00:07:02,803 --> 00:07:05,799
It means the recommendation has
earned its place in the next step.

96
00:07:06,641 --> 00:07:07,800
We can still gather evidence.

97
00:07:08,512 --> 00:07:11,178
We cannot quietly turn the absence
of evidence into permission.

98
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:17,008
Start with the reference because
a plausible citation is often

99
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:20,292
the first thing that makes
an answer look trustworthy.

100
00:07:21,661 --> 00:07:24,625
Resolving the reference
and then its claim.

101
00:07:25,634 --> 00:07:28,417
A filled source field is not
the same thing as support.

102
00:07:29,488 --> 00:07:31,534
A plausible link can point nowhere.

103
00:07:32,427 --> 00:07:34,122
A real document can be wrong version.

104
00:07:34,701 --> 00:07:37,009
The right version can say
something unrelated to the claim.

105
00:07:38,822 --> 00:07:43,907
Let's take this example The first
line says that it tried to resolve

106
00:07:43,907 --> 00:07:49,080
a retry policy against revision R7,
and it found that it doesn't exist.

107
00:07:49,957 --> 00:07:56,802
Read- The second line says that it tried
to resolve a demo runbook at revision R7.

108
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:03,087
The agent found it, and that
describes rollback steps.

109
00:08:04,474 --> 00:08:07,294
But that does not establish
that the error rate fell.

110
00:08:08,648 --> 00:08:11,075
Finding the document answers
an existence question.

111
00:08:11,801 --> 00:08:14,098
It does not answer the
engineering question we need.

112
00:08:14,924 --> 00:08:17,128
So what's the learning?

113
00:08:17,965 --> 00:08:21,217
Different failures and
different next fits.

114
00:08:22,554 --> 00:08:23,629
Keep the checks separate.

115
00:08:24,629 --> 00:08:25,542
Open the source.

116
00:08:26,651 --> 00:08:27,703
Pin the exact version.

117
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:29,299
Match the exact scope.

118
00:08:30,511 --> 00:08:32,709
Then ask whether it
supports an actual claim.

119
00:08:34,551 --> 00:08:36,299
Let's take an example for a code review.

120
00:08:37,070 --> 00:08:41,299
That means a commit, a changed
code, and a test that asserts

121
00:08:41,305 --> 00:08:42,601
the behavior being claimed.

122
00:08:44,032 --> 00:08:47,002
A test with a nearby
name, it's not enough.

123
00:08:47,861 --> 00:08:49,180
Some checks are mechanical, though.

124
00:08:50,538 --> 00:08:53,080
A fingerprint can tell us
that the source changed.

125
00:08:53,824 --> 00:08:57,045
It cannot tell us that the
source is truthful or relevant.

126
00:08:57,953 --> 00:08:59,265
Those questions still need a human.

127
00:09:00,612 --> 00:09:05,119
Once the claim has been copied into
other work, even a correct correction

128
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:06,304
becomes a different problem.

129
00:09:07,284 --> 00:09:12,218
The next failure is a wrong fact becoming
the input to several later answers.

130
00:09:13,176 --> 00:09:16,329
Take the claim at revision one,
the unsupported rate claim.

131
00:09:17,570 --> 00:09:20,674
The triage note cites
revision one of our claim.

132
00:09:21,545 --> 00:09:25,252
The review note picks
it up and then uses it.

133
00:09:25,824 --> 00:09:29,579
The runbook note uses it
as a reason to proceed.

134
00:09:31,182 --> 00:09:35,993
The three artifacts now agree,
but they do not contain three

135
00:09:35,993 --> 00:09:37,053
independent observations.

136
00:09:37,664 --> 00:09:39,959
They all depend on the
same unsupported sentence.

137
00:09:41,091 --> 00:09:44,463
That repetition can make the
claim look stronger, but it can

138
00:09:44,503 --> 00:09:47,820
also bring the old sentence back
after someone has corrected it.

139
00:09:49,019 --> 00:09:54,040
Now, someone went and fixed that
claim and created a new revision, R2.

140
00:09:55,271 --> 00:10:00,278
But creating a new revision does not
repair those dependencies by itself.

141
00:10:01,946 --> 00:10:06,502
The old artifacts at revision one,
they still exist, and they need to

142
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:08,667
be blocked until they are rebuilt.

143
00:10:09,909 --> 00:10:14,538
The next step must not treat revision
one as current simply because

144
00:10:14,564 --> 00:10:15,727
the old text is still readable.

145
00:10:16,766 --> 00:10:18,627
This is different from
evidence getting old.

146
00:10:19,627 --> 00:10:24,510
The old claim has been replaced,
even if it was written at any time.

147
00:10:26,002 --> 00:10:30,507
A useful correction names what
changed, why it changed, and

148
00:10:30,507 --> 00:10:31,802
the evidence that replaced it.

149
00:10:32,654 --> 00:10:35,647
The test is to-- not whether
the agent agrees with us.

150
00:10:36,302 --> 00:10:39,387
The test is whether the old fact
can still drive the next action.

151
00:10:40,766 --> 00:10:43,494
There is another way a
fact can stop being usable.

152
00:10:44,329 --> 00:10:46,898
Over the time Long sessions.

153
00:10:48,089 --> 00:10:51,165
Many of us use sessions for a long time.

154
00:10:52,165 --> 00:10:56,521
And long session drift is not only
forgetting an instruction, it is

155
00:10:56,527 --> 00:10:59,913
also carrying an old observation
forward without carrying the

156
00:10:59,913 --> 00:11:01,182
conditions that made it useful.

157
00:11:02,112 --> 00:11:08,003
It can be due to drift or agent
compacting its conversation.

158
00:11:09,389 --> 00:11:10,383
Let's take an illustration.

159
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:16,064
A sample data window
ends at nine oh five UTC.

160
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:19,603
The data as of is nine oh five.

161
00:11:20,864 --> 00:11:22,411
We read that at nine oh six.

162
00:11:23,992 --> 00:11:26,153
And the first edition is at nine oh seven.

163
00:11:27,153 --> 00:11:33,055
For this sample, we chose a
five-minute freshness limit.

164
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,656
The data is young enough
for that first edition.

165
00:11:37,706 --> 00:11:39,096
Notice which clock matters.

166
00:11:39,478 --> 00:11:43,685
The data's as of time, not just when
the data-- when the read returned.

167
00:11:44,475 --> 00:11:51,938
At nine fifteen, another edition came
in, but the freshness limit expired,

168
00:11:52,941 --> 00:11:55,997
so it, it requires another reread.

169
00:11:57,897 --> 00:12:00,305
The five minutes here is not
a universal recommendation.

170
00:12:01,276 --> 00:12:06,704
A frozen source file and a live health
signal have different useful lifetimes.

171
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:10,359
The right li-- the right
limit depends on the edition.

172
00:12:11,650 --> 00:12:15,247
A change commit or target can
also invalidate old evidences,

173
00:12:16,134 --> 00:12:17,427
even if it was recent enough.

174
00:12:18,661 --> 00:12:23,098
The rule is to check the age and
scope when the evidence is used.

175
00:12:23,797 --> 00:12:29,474
A cache lookup doesn't reset its age, and
a later successful read doesn't rewrite

176
00:12:29,522 --> 00:12:30,901
what was known at the earlier edition.

177
00:12:32,144 --> 00:12:34,393
We need a record that
carries those decisions.

178
00:12:35,407 --> 00:12:41,140
If you see the illustrative freshness
check, essentially the edition time when

179
00:12:41,140 --> 00:12:44,766
it happened minus the data as of time.

180
00:12:45,201 --> 00:12:48,326
If it's greater than our freshness
limit, in this case, five

181
00:12:48,329 --> 00:12:50,125
minutes, the edition is stale.

182
00:12:51,118 --> 00:12:55,129
Now, let's replace the
unsupported sentence with a

183
00:12:55,132 --> 00:12:57,021
record another engineer can check.

184
00:12:58,542 --> 00:13:02,395
In this illustration, revision
two of the claim points to

185
00:13:02,395 --> 00:13:04,176
the new evidence, E seventy.

186
00:13:05,243 --> 00:13:13,027
We keep the source version, hash,
the service, the retry policy, the

187
00:13:13,029 --> 00:13:15,826
two samples, and the time window.

188
00:13:16,826 --> 00:13:18,565
If you see, there are
three different times.

189
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:24,800
The as of time, the read at
time, the evaluated at time.

190
00:13:25,823 --> 00:13:27,930
Three answer three different questions.

191
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:31,688
The result is established negative.

192
00:13:32,688 --> 00:13:36,435
The question was whether the error
rate decreased, and the evidence

193
00:13:36,437 --> 00:13:37,938
does not support that improvement.

194
00:13:39,415 --> 00:13:42,318
Let's see What this lets us check.

195
00:13:43,587 --> 00:13:49,160
This tells the next reader, inspect the
same source and ask the same question.

196
00:13:50,144 --> 00:13:55,294
It also lets a reviewer reconstruct what
was known when the decision was made.

197
00:13:56,505 --> 00:13:58,871
Carrying a snapshot and
carrying a reference.

198
00:13:59,865 --> 00:14:02,901
A snapshot travels with
the decision, but can age.

199
00:14:03,951 --> 00:14:07,608
A reference keeps the source separate,
but may fail to resolve later.

200
00:14:08,602 --> 00:14:11,014
That representation cushion
is still being discussed.

201
00:14:11,593 --> 00:14:13,666
Either way, we don't lose
the meaning of the input.

202
00:14:14,443 --> 00:14:17,471
An unknown decision is not always false.

203
00:14:18,289 --> 00:14:19,401
Let's take these cases

204
00:14:20,401 --> 00:14:28,805
If the state is established positive, it
says that we check the condition and it's

205
00:14:28,806 --> 00:14:35,806
satisfied If the state is established
negative, it says that we check the

206
00:14:35,807 --> 00:14:38,517
condition, and it's not satisfied.

207
00:14:39,516 --> 00:14:43,686
A complete comparison showing
no rate reduction is a negative

208
00:14:43,686 --> 00:14:44,935
result for the improvement claim.

209
00:14:45,412 --> 00:14:46,454
It is not a missing result.

210
00:14:47,454 --> 00:14:49,478
Now, not established.

211
00:14:51,011 --> 00:14:53,435
It means that we didn't
get the required input.

212
00:14:54,493 --> 00:14:59,242
Still, it means that we have a value,
but it is too old for this decision.

213
00:15:00,358 --> 00:15:05,429
Not applicable, it means the policy
does not require that input here.

214
00:15:07,205 --> 00:15:10,002
The last state, it's not an escape hatch.

215
00:15:10,533 --> 00:15:14,321
The agent can choose whenever it
feels that the check is inconvenient.

216
00:15:14,841 --> 00:15:17,729
Imagine a triage probe timing out.

217
00:15:18,492 --> 00:15:21,693
We should not turn the timeout
into either healthy or unhealthy.

218
00:15:22,857 --> 00:15:25,771
We can refuse to close the
investigation while still recording

219
00:15:25,772 --> 00:15:27,492
the health input was unavailable.

220
00:15:28,663 --> 00:15:32,127
That sends the next person towards a
failed read, not a made-up health finding.

221
00:15:32,593 --> 00:15:36,460
A separate policy uses these input
states to decide what may proceed.

222
00:15:37,575 --> 00:15:43,099
And uncertainty about an action's observed
effect belongs on a separate axis.

223
00:15:44,584 --> 00:15:46,535
We'll come back to that
after the action boundary.

224
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:51,974
Keeping the failure reasons distinct
The final answer might be the same

225
00:15:51,981 --> 00:15:55,990
refusal, but the reason why it
failed points to different work.

226
00:15:56,624 --> 00:15:57,841
Let's take the same states.

227
00:15:59,570 --> 00:16:00,392
Not established.

228
00:16:02,048 --> 00:16:08,926
It says that there's no usable probe,
and it means that repair, repair or

229
00:16:08,957 --> 00:16:11,526
repeat the read An established negative.

230
00:16:13,469 --> 00:16:15,653
There was an observed predicate failure.

231
00:16:17,568 --> 00:16:22,850
And observed negative means
inspected finding Stale state.

232
00:16:23,849 --> 00:16:31,301
It says that value is too old and we
need to refresh it at the point of use.

233
00:16:31,970 --> 00:16:35,955
Effect observation is different
again than input observation.

234
00:16:36,877 --> 00:16:41,577
A no matching observation does not
prove success, and the silence does

235
00:16:41,607 --> 00:16:43,345
not automatically prove failure.

236
00:16:44,165 --> 00:16:46,005
Read back using the action's identity.

237
00:16:46,916 --> 00:16:52,021
If the effect still cannot be determined,
keep that uncertainty and reconcile it.

238
00:16:53,077 --> 00:16:55,461
That is where indeterminate belongs.

239
00:16:56,359 --> 00:17:02,150
The uncertain observed fact,
not another input state The

240
00:17:02,154 --> 00:17:03,531
record tells us what failed.

241
00:17:04,104 --> 00:17:07,055
Something outside the record
still has to enforce the refusal.

242
00:17:08,351 --> 00:17:13,127
So we shouldn't put gates
and checks inside the prompt.

243
00:17:13,218 --> 00:17:14,500
We should put it outside the prompt.

244
00:17:15,101 --> 00:17:15,357
And we do.

245
00:17:17,002 --> 00:17:22,099
If the same model can skip a check
and declare it passed, we have a

246
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:23,940
request, not an enforced boundary.

247
00:17:25,774 --> 00:17:27,311
Let's take this short Python code.

248
00:17:28,591 --> 00:17:29,822
It shows a recommendation gate.

249
00:17:30,617 --> 00:17:32,546
The caller owns the required checks.

250
00:17:32,995 --> 00:17:38,015
If any required result is not established
positive, return a refusal and its reason.

251
00:17:39,323 --> 00:17:43,060
If all checks pass, the
recommendation can go to human review.

252
00:17:43,815 --> 00:17:45,322
It has not become an authorized write.

253
00:17:46,783 --> 00:17:50,376
so always check before emitting it.

254
00:17:51,990 --> 00:17:54,676
We need to check the source,
its scope, and freshness.

255
00:17:55,650 --> 00:18:00,946
We need to recompute the math
and deal with the contradictions.

256
00:18:02,028 --> 00:18:06,326
Conflicting observations can
stay unresolved until someone or

257
00:18:06,334 --> 00:18:07,753
something resolves the conflict.

258
00:18:08,706 --> 00:18:11,519
A complete negative finding
keeps its own reason.

259
00:18:12,229 --> 00:18:15,347
Neither path should emit a
positive recommendation with a

260
00:18:15,347 --> 00:18:17,575
warning paragraph underneath it.

261
00:18:19,307 --> 00:18:23,859
This positive only rule is the policy
of our example, not a universal rule.

262
00:18:25,008 --> 00:18:27,392
The small offline example
exercises branches.

263
00:18:28,210 --> 00:18:32,981
A real system also needs trusted
readers, identity, authoritative

264
00:18:32,984 --> 00:18:36,536
policy, and enforcement on every
route that can use the result.

265
00:18:37,750 --> 00:18:42,409
That includes the route where someone
copies rejected text into the next step.

266
00:18:43,337 --> 00:18:46,523
Logging a refusal is not enough
if the next step can ignore it.

267
00:18:47,622 --> 00:18:51,533
Now, let's compute the math to the gate.

268
00:18:52,550 --> 00:18:54,461
Let's take this sample.

269
00:18:55,863 --> 00:18:57,860
We have a baseline and a candidate.

270
00:18:58,860 --> 00:19:02,058
Baseline has ten errors
in thousand requests.

271
00:19:02,572 --> 00:19:05,489
The candidate has six errors
in six hundred requests.

272
00:19:05,976 --> 00:19:08,663
Before calculating anything,
we clean up the data.

273
00:19:08,788 --> 00:19:12,237
Reject any missing samples,
partial reads, zero denominators.

274
00:19:13,995 --> 00:19:17,712
If you take raw error count,
you'll see that it went from ten

275
00:19:17,715 --> 00:19:19,581
to six, a forty percent reduction.

276
00:19:20,965 --> 00:19:27,262
But if you calculate the error rate,
in baseline it's ten out of thousand,

277
00:19:27,936 --> 00:19:32,767
one percent, and in candidate it's
six out of six hundred, one percent.

278
00:19:33,919 --> 00:19:38,847
The error rate didn't change, and
the rate reduction is zero percent.

279
00:19:39,905 --> 00:19:41,526
This does not mean the error rate is zero.

280
00:19:41,948 --> 00:19:44,197
It's just that the error
rates didn't change.

281
00:19:45,971 --> 00:19:49,653
The correct claim is no rate
reduction in the sample.

282
00:19:51,052 --> 00:19:53,396
Do not recommend the configuration
change on the improvement claim.

283
00:19:54,436 --> 00:19:58,473
That is narrower than saying the
change is bad or should never be used.

284
00:19:59,544 --> 00:20:02,774
The small comparison is not a
significant test or a causal experiment.

285
00:20:03,654 --> 00:20:06,799
We have replaced an unsupported
percentage with measurements,

286
00:20:07,482 --> 00:20:09,571
a calculation, and addition.

287
00:20:10,918 --> 00:20:14,155
The correction has to change what
the next step is allowed to use.

288
00:20:14,634 --> 00:20:17,366
Now we have a new revision.

289
00:20:18,331 --> 00:20:23,451
This record names the claim, its new
revision, the old revision it replaces,

290
00:20:23,808 --> 00:20:26,093
the reason, and the replacement evidence.

291
00:20:27,610 --> 00:20:31,890
It also says that if you consume
the old revision, reject it.

292
00:20:32,736 --> 00:20:37,074
The old text can still exist
for audit or explanation without

293
00:20:37,082 --> 00:20:38,673
remaining as a valid input.

294
00:20:39,274 --> 00:20:42,918
Now you have a new revision,
but how do you make sure it's

295
00:20:42,918 --> 00:20:46,312
correctly enforced and deployed?

296
00:20:47,285 --> 00:20:51,933
We need to first block the
revision R1 in all dependencies.

297
00:20:53,407 --> 00:21:01,350
And we need to rebuild C1 at revision two
with the evidence E17 across everything.

298
00:21:02,206 --> 00:21:06,639
And that gives us a concrete answer
to the pattern where agent defends

299
00:21:06,642 --> 00:21:10,534
a claim, accepts a correction, and
then repeats the old claim later.

300
00:21:11,232 --> 00:21:16,476
And recheck the revision and
claim before next using it.

301
00:21:17,491 --> 00:21:19,634
Our example keeps a small dependency list.

302
00:21:20,071 --> 00:21:23,520
A real artifact with no recorded
dependencies can be rebuilt automatically.

303
00:21:24,985 --> 00:21:27,985
That is a gap to expose, not a reason
to assume the correction reached it.

304
00:21:29,168 --> 00:21:34,968
Let's take the telemetry example that
did not justify the configuration change.

305
00:21:35,872 --> 00:21:38,849
This next branch starts only
after the evidence checks pass.

306
00:21:39,641 --> 00:21:43,390
If you've seen this illustration,
approval is for a specific operation,

307
00:21:43,796 --> 00:21:49,943
a single API in a staging environment,
and with a specific retry limit.

308
00:21:51,653 --> 00:21:59,783
And the grant that binds these are
a specific approver, an action ID,

309
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:03,890
a payload digest, and an expiration
time, and a single use rule.

310
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:09,828
If we change the scope from staging to
production, the approval is invalidated

311
00:22:11,800 --> 00:22:15,220
And changing the text of an external
update, it needs a new approval.

312
00:22:16,219 --> 00:22:18,864
A consumed approval cannot
authorize the next write.

313
00:22:20,139 --> 00:22:24,714
This is why approval has to be
checked at the tool boundary, not

314
00:22:24,723 --> 00:22:26,369
just remembered from an LDN message.

315
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:30,612
The request or its evidence
may have changed while waiting

316
00:22:30,614 --> 00:22:31,835
for someone to approve it.

317
00:22:33,460 --> 00:22:38,265
The example demonstrates matching the
approved request and refusing reuse.

318
00:22:38,979 --> 00:22:42,411
Authenticating approvers,
persisting grants, and handling

319
00:22:42,435 --> 00:22:44,887
two simultaneous attempts are
additional work for any system.

320
00:22:45,849 --> 00:22:48,987
Pausing and resuming the conversation
doesn't remove any of those checks.

321
00:22:50,032 --> 00:22:52,460
We regularly pause sessions
and then resume it.

322
00:22:53,765 --> 00:22:56,969
And pausing and resuming shouldn't
carry over the approvals.

323
00:22:57,570 --> 00:22:58,664
Take this sample.

324
00:23:00,367 --> 00:23:04,197
Keep the action and its target,
but check the current grant and

325
00:23:04,197 --> 00:23:05,574
the age of its inputs again.

326
00:23:06,795 --> 00:23:08,408
Here, the revocation check is unavailable.

327
00:23:09,239 --> 00:23:13,381
Our example's policy refuses the
write and reports the failed check.

328
00:23:14,542 --> 00:23:17,012
An old conversation is not
evidence of a current permission.

329
00:23:18,012 --> 00:23:19,403
Recheck before the next write.

330
00:23:20,546 --> 00:23:25,042
The linked resumption requirement
separates checking an interrupted

331
00:23:25,043 --> 00:23:28,174
participant from termination
and revocation checks.

332
00:23:29,044 --> 00:23:31,076
It also calls for failures to be reported.

333
00:23:32,076 --> 00:23:35,952
The practical rule is simple:
restore context, not side effects.

334
00:23:36,854 --> 00:23:39,845
Don't resubmit a write just
because a session resumed.

335
00:23:40,869 --> 00:23:43,535
A later successful check
can support a new decision.

336
00:23:44,284 --> 00:23:46,554
It doesn't retroactively
authorize earlier ones.

337
00:23:47,375 --> 00:23:50,831
Even after an authorized action
is submitted, there is another

338
00:23:50,839 --> 00:23:54,958
boundary to check, and a
submission is not confirmation.

339
00:23:55,763 --> 00:23:57,269
Let's take this illustration.

340
00:23:58,725 --> 00:24:01,859
It says that evaluation is completed.

341
00:24:02,662 --> 00:24:04,025
The policy permitted the action.

342
00:24:05,148 --> 00:24:09,948
The operation was accepted, but
its effect is not yet confirmed.

343
00:24:10,188 --> 00:24:11,157
It's not observed yet.

344
00:24:12,294 --> 00:24:16,176
And this is a u-unusual
normal asynchronous state.

345
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:20,959
A later read has to match the
action and the intended effect

346
00:24:21,001 --> 00:24:22,174
before we can call it confirmed.

347
00:24:23,018 --> 00:24:25,354
So what each signal actually says.

348
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:28,102
Submitted means we sent something.

349
00:24:28,869 --> 00:24:31,314
Accepted means something admitted it.

350
00:24:32,509 --> 00:24:36,854
Executed is a component
reporting its own work.

351
00:24:37,932 --> 00:24:42,590
Confirmed needs a separate
observation appropriate to the claim.

352
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:45,848
Reading back a retry setting
confirms that setting.

353
00:24:47,639 --> 00:24:50,007
It doesn't prove that every
worker has picked it up or that

354
00:24:50,008 --> 00:24:51,264
users are seeing fewer errors.

355
00:24:52,354 --> 00:24:55,428
An agent observation doesn't
automatically mean the action failed.

356
00:24:56,735 --> 00:24:58,029
The observation path may have failed.

357
00:24:59,837 --> 00:25:03,607
Let's go to the next example
where I'll show this distinction

358
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:05,049
in one of the public repos.

359
00:25:05,924 --> 00:25:10,554
A completed task doesn't mean
it's an accepted notification.

360
00:25:11,443 --> 00:25:17,378
Let's take this sample from a regression
test, an A2A open source repo.

361
00:25:18,555 --> 00:25:23,086
If you read the diagram from top
to bottom, there are three separate

362
00:25:23,112 --> 00:25:28,775
participants: a task state, a HTTP
sender, and a reject endpoint.

363
00:25:30,496 --> 00:25:34,086
The task reaches its completed
state, and it's persisted.

364
00:25:35,379 --> 00:25:37,863
Its events give the sender
delivery work to do.

365
00:25:38,464 --> 00:25:41,262
Completing the task does not mean
the callback has, have been accepted.

366
00:25:43,222 --> 00:25:44,881
The sender attempts the delivery.

367
00:25:45,867 --> 00:25:51,577
The endpoint returns either service
unavailable or redirect The test

368
00:25:52,396 --> 00:25:55,536
waits for the boundary attempts to
drain before checking the final state.

369
00:25:57,135 --> 00:25:57,866
See the bottom remark.

370
00:25:58,688 --> 00:26:00,407
It has four separate observations.

371
00:26:01,287 --> 00:26:02,422
The task is still completed.

372
00:26:03,210 --> 00:26:04,130
The attempts are drained.

373
00:26:05,046 --> 00:26:08,817
There are zero accept notifications,
and the registration is read.

374
00:26:09,774 --> 00:26:11,886
The attempts did reach endpoint.

375
00:26:12,347 --> 00:26:14,596
Zero means the notification
handler accepted none.

376
00:26:15,601 --> 00:26:17,169
It does not mean that no requests are in.

377
00:26:18,294 --> 00:26:21,373
This fixture configured
two attempts per event.

378
00:26:22,259 --> 00:26:23,231
Redirect gets one.

379
00:26:24,390 --> 00:26:29,433
With three persisted events that produce
six requests for service unavailable or

380
00:26:29,465 --> 00:26:32,331
three for redirects So here's the lesson.

381
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:36,096
Task completion doesn't prove
delivery, and failed delivery doesn't

382
00:26:36,098 --> 00:26:40,555
authorize the sender to write task
state or remove the registration.

383
00:26:41,409 --> 00:26:46,248
So let's dig into the
test I was talking about.

384
00:26:47,408 --> 00:26:48,758
The assertions behind the diagram.

385
00:26:49,635 --> 00:26:50,975
Let's take this sample code.

386
00:26:52,658 --> 00:26:54,841
It waits for the delivery work to drain.

387
00:26:55,505 --> 00:26:56,658
It checks that count.

388
00:26:57,243 --> 00:26:59,973
It checks that accepted
notification collection is empty.

389
00:27:00,807 --> 00:27:04,007
It reads the task again and
reads the registration again.

390
00:27:05,308 --> 00:27:09,420
Each assertion names the state it
observes and says that remote business

391
00:27:09,435 --> 00:27:11,812
process received or processed the event.

392
00:27:12,149 --> 00:27:12,407
Need it.

393
00:27:13,416 --> 00:27:14,657
Both failure branches matter.

394
00:27:15,231 --> 00:27:18,894
The source link includes the setup
and the different attempt counts,

395
00:27:19,851 --> 00:27:22,307
not just this shortened accept.

396
00:27:23,004 --> 00:27:25,085
The delivery documentation
supplies the limits.

397
00:27:25,556 --> 00:27:31,377
Task persistence and queue
admission are separate operations,

398
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:32,807
not one atomic transaction.

399
00:27:33,681 --> 00:27:37,230
A durable sender alone would not
close the crash window before

400
00:27:37,234 --> 00:27:38,296
an event enters the queue.

401
00:27:39,252 --> 00:27:42,254
The documented queue is in
memory, and the items are finite.

402
00:27:42,907 --> 00:27:46,531
This does not establish guaranteed
receipt, restart durability, or

403
00:27:46,536 --> 00:27:48,727
exactly one, exactly once possible.

404
00:27:49,689 --> 00:27:55,783
So completed proves delivery is not
supported by this code, and delivery

405
00:27:55,788 --> 00:27:59,912
failure preserves task state and
the registration under this test is

406
00:27:59,915 --> 00:28:01,367
a smaller claim than we can check.

407
00:28:02,407 --> 00:28:03,501
That's a code review pattern.

408
00:28:04,072 --> 00:28:07,169
Read what the assertion
actually proves and keep the

409
00:28:07,169 --> 00:28:08,666
implementation limits next to it.

410
00:28:09,659 --> 00:28:10,373
That's the mechanism.

411
00:28:11,173 --> 00:28:13,118
Now, let's recap.

412
00:28:13,906 --> 00:28:19,860
In this last part, I'll bring back the
four practical questions, what each

413
00:28:19,861 --> 00:28:26,085
control can do, what I would replace, how
to test it, and where the example stops.

414
00:28:26,832 --> 00:28:31,312
A prompt can request sources,
checks, and corrections.

415
00:28:31,983 --> 00:28:36,897
It is a useful guidance, but it does
not block unsupported output by itself.

416
00:28:38,370 --> 00:28:42,731
A human review can judge relevance
and approve an exact action.

417
00:28:43,194 --> 00:28:46,219
That still doesn't prove that every
later step will honor the decision.

418
00:28:47,197 --> 00:28:50,683
An executable gate can reject
the wrong scope, an old

419
00:28:50,684 --> 00:28:52,225
revision, or missing evidence.

420
00:28:52,806 --> 00:28:54,619
It cannot make an untruthful source true.

421
00:28:55,619 --> 00:28:59,539
An independent effect observation
can confirm a specific effect.

422
00:29:00,097 --> 00:29:02,910
It cannot justify a wider
result that nobody observed

423
00:29:03,910 --> 00:29:05,592
These controls also have costs.

424
00:29:06,304 --> 00:29:07,062
Reads take time.

425
00:29:07,803 --> 00:29:08,979
Refusals need an owner.

426
00:29:09,439 --> 00:29:11,178
Revision tracking needs maintenance.

427
00:29:11,611 --> 00:29:14,752
The observation path can fail
separately from the action.

428
00:29:15,594 --> 00:29:18,046
Those are the design choices
and trade-offs to make openly.

429
00:29:18,989 --> 00:29:21,721
Not reasons to collapse everything
into a single done field.

430
00:29:22,521 --> 00:29:26,261
Name the control you have, and don't
give it credit for a stronger one.

431
00:29:26,899 --> 00:29:29,019
More activity is not
necessarily a stronger control.

432
00:29:30,019 --> 00:29:33,021
Instead of another reminder to
remember the rule, use a check that

433
00:29:33,022 --> 00:29:34,620
the next step actually has to pass.

434
00:29:35,878 --> 00:29:38,971
Give it a named input, a
result, and a refusal path.

435
00:29:39,650 --> 00:29:42,740
If it cannot establish a required
input, stop or route the work.

436
00:29:43,266 --> 00:29:45,267
Don't fill the gap with
more confident text.

437
00:29:46,110 --> 00:29:48,598
Instead of testing only the
latest failure, keep the

438
00:29:48,598 --> 00:29:49,772
accumulated regression cases.

439
00:29:50,677 --> 00:29:52,608
And stop reviewing until it feels right.

440
00:29:52,948 --> 00:29:55,341
Define the exit criteria
and the review budget.

441
00:29:55,889 --> 00:29:59,689
If the criteria aren't met, name
the unresolved issue and its owner.

442
00:30:00,226 --> 00:30:02,512
A budget is not permission
to ignore what remains.

443
00:30:03,826 --> 00:30:10,127
These are choices I would use to evaluate
a workflow, not a claim that one practice

444
00:30:10,168 --> 00:30:12,110
permanently solves all the problems.

445
00:30:12,937 --> 00:30:14,641
For each control, ask three questions.

446
00:30:15,101 --> 00:30:16,293
What must stay true?

447
00:30:17,038 --> 00:30:17,979
Where do we test it?

448
00:30:18,248 --> 00:30:20,992
And what happens when
the check can't finish?

449
00:30:22,421 --> 00:30:25,887
That gives the next engineer
something more useful than, "The

450
00:30:25,888 --> 00:30:27,724
agent sounded confident this time."

451
00:30:28,881 --> 00:30:32,170
Turn the failure cases into
inputs and expected results.

452
00:30:33,117 --> 00:30:33,979
Remove the source.

453
00:30:34,629 --> 00:30:35,433
Change the scope.

454
00:30:36,192 --> 00:30:39,002
Return a partial read or
conflicting observations.

455
00:30:39,903 --> 00:30:40,841
Advance the clock.

456
00:30:41,569 --> 00:30:43,691
Try to reuse a claim
that has been replaced.

457
00:30:44,408 --> 00:30:46,745
Missing inputs should stay unresolved.

458
00:30:47,271 --> 00:30:49,585
Old evidence should be marked stale.

459
00:30:50,220 --> 00:30:52,073
A superseded claim should be rejected.

460
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:57,455
Use of match counts and check that
the rate improvement claim is refused.

461
00:30:58,061 --> 00:31:01,629
Change the request after approval or
try to consume the approval twice,

462
00:31:01,963 --> 00:31:02,899
the authorization should fail.

463
00:31:04,788 --> 00:31:08,538
Accept an operation, but
withhold its effect observation.

464
00:31:09,194 --> 00:31:10,675
The effect should remain unconfirmed.

465
00:31:11,579 --> 00:31:13,669
A small offline example
passes these checks.

466
00:31:14,086 --> 00:31:16,835
That is evidence about the example, not
a measurement of production reliability.

467
00:31:17,828 --> 00:31:21,408
For a real workflow in production,
test the ways around the boundary.

468
00:31:21,949 --> 00:31:25,616
A step that ignores the validator, a
document with no dependency record,

469
00:31:26,279 --> 00:31:29,748
a stale read after approval, or an
observation for the wrong action.

470
00:31:30,836 --> 00:31:34,854
Start with one condition that matters,
one exact input, and one refusal

471
00:31:35,328 --> 00:31:36,380
that must survive the next step.

472
00:31:37,121 --> 00:31:40,120
A test that can show that boundary
failing is more useful than a

473
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:41,616
checklist that says to be careful.

474
00:31:42,525 --> 00:31:45,089
The example also has
lim-limits we should name.

475
00:31:46,033 --> 00:31:51,372
It has fixed synthetic reads, explicit
refusals, the rate calculation,

476
00:31:51,638 --> 00:31:52,786
and a small dependency list.

477
00:31:53,399 --> 00:31:57,701
It shows how to match an approved
request, reject reuse, and tie an

478
00:31:58,069 --> 00:31:59,706
effect observation into an action.

479
00:32:01,672 --> 00:32:03,994
It does not authenticate
real people or services.

480
00:32:04,195 --> 00:32:06,625
It does not provide durable
state, atomic approval.

481
00:32:06,985 --> 00:32:09,535
Just as clear about the boundary
around the evidence bucket

482
00:32:10,535 --> 00:32:13,077
So these are some of the public
sources behind this talk.

483
00:32:14,382 --> 00:32:22,084
It came from my discussions in IETF Agent
Proto charter, my own Internet draft,

484
00:32:22,621 --> 00:32:24,484
and my-- some of my open source work.

485
00:32:25,249 --> 00:32:28,120
And this brings us back
to the three boundaries.

486
00:32:28,789 --> 00:32:33,836
Evidence before recommendation,
approval before action, observation

487
00:32:33,842 --> 00:32:35,805
after it Tak for det, Ruan

